// `wild:tool-provider` — Tier-2 tool-pack capability surface.
//
// Plugin Concept P3 (W2). A tool-provider plugin exports a list of
// LLM-callable tools (math evaluation, datetime arithmetic, unit
// conversion, …) plus an `invoke` entry point that dispatches a
// JSON-shaped argument bundle to the named tool. The host
// `ToolProviderRegistry` aggregates `list-tools()` across every
// loaded tool-provider plugin into a single flat catalog.
//
// Who reaches that catalog — it is NOT every agent (see
// `docs/plugin-developer-guide.md` § "Who invokes a provider tool"):
//   - WORKERS via `wild:tools/invoke` — the generic LLM tool-caller.
//   - the ELDER via the tool's SKILL — ship `list-skill-mds()` and the
//     host fronts the tool through the `MergedSkillRegistry`.
//   - the CHIEF only via `wild:tool-routing/invoke` for deterministic
//     ADR-0039 schedule recipes; it is NOT a generic tool-caller.
//   - operators directly via `wild tool invoke <name> <args-json>`
//     (the LLM-free smoke-test surface).
//
// Vocabulary — a *tool* is not a *tool-provider* (1:N, by design):
//
//   - A **tool** is ONE named verb: a `tool-spec` with its own
//     `json-schema` (and, per ADR-0055, its own input/output signature).
//     `math-eval` and `http-fetch` are tools.
//   - A **tool-provider** is ONE component that exposes one-OR-MANY tools
//     via `list-tools()` and dispatches them by `name` through `invoke`.
//     `math-tools` provides THREE (`math-eval` / `datetime-arith` /
//     `unit-convert`); `http-fetcher` provides TWO.
//
// The plural lives in the contents, not the name — which is why this
// contract is `tool-provider`, not `tool`: a component is a pack of
// tools, never a single one. The consumer-side `wild:tools` is plural
// for the same reason — a caller goes through the aggregated catalog of
// every provider, not one.
//
// Composition (provider-flavor):
//
//   world tool-provider-plugin {
//       include wild:plugin-meta/plugin-base@0.3.0;
//       import wild:secrets/store@0.1.0;
//       import wasi:http/outgoing-handler@0.2.0;
//       import wild:blobs/store@0.4.0;
//       export wild:tool-provider/tools@0.4.0;
//   }
//
// `math-tools` is the reference implementation: pure-eval, no
// imports beyond `plugin-meta`. A future `web-search-tools` would
// bind `wasi:http/outgoing-handler`; an `s3-tools` would bind
// `wild:blobs/store` + `wild:secrets/store`. Whether each import
// is granted depends on the plugin's trust tier (D3 Stage-2 gate).
//
// ADR-0059 — a component needing an API key / token imports
// `wild:secrets/store@0.1.0` and reads it at use-time via
// `get(alias)` (never a literal, never a config key). The blueprint
// `## Secrets` block declares the alias (SCREAMING_SNAKE); the host
// maps it to the operator's stored secret. Wright (the forge coder)
// is instructed to generate exactly this; see `prompts/chief-default/
// wright.md` § Secrets.
//
// JSON over the WIT boundary, deliberately:
//
//   - `tool-spec.json-schema` is a JSON Schema string. The host
//     ships it through to the LLM verbatim so adding a new tool
//     doesn't require a WIT bump every time the schema changes.
//   - `invoke(name, args-json)` takes JSON-string args + returns
//     a JSON-string result. Same logic — the LLM produces JSON,
//     consumes JSON, and a per-tool typed Wasm record would force
//     a WIT redeploy on every tool addition.
//   - `tool-error` is a typed variant so the host can distinguish
//     "the tool doesn't exist" (route bug) from "the args don't
//     match the schema" (LLM bug) from "the tool ran and crashed"
//     (plugin bug). The `wild:ai/chat` tool-result loop can choose
//     to recover or surface the failure to the LLM accordingly.

// @0.3.0 (ADR-0100): `invoke` became an `async func` so a tool can drain a
// component-model-async `stream<u8>` import (e.g. `wild:host-source/reader`)
// while decoding a large source in place. The host drives every `invoke`
// under `run_concurrent`; the sync `list-tools`/`list-skill-mds` are
// unchanged. Requires a wit-bindgen 0.58 guest.
//
// @0.4.0 (ADR-0116 D1): `tool-error` gained the `fault(fault-detail)`
// case — a failure whose CLASS the tool knows (401 → `auth`, 429 →
// `rate-limited`) travels typed instead of as prose inside
// `execution-failed`, so the host waist can route on it. Additive for
// plugin code (the old cases still compile + dispatch), but the
// tool-error member set changed, so the minor bumps (house rule; the
// package sits in the wit-internal.lock publish allow-list).
package wild:tool-provider@0.4.0;

interface tools {
    /// One tool the plugin makes available. The host renders these
    /// into the `tools: [...]` array on every `wild:ai/chat`
    /// invocation, so name + description + json-schema are LLM-
    /// facing strings.
    record tool-spec {
        /// Stable tool name. Lower-kebab-case by convention.
        /// Globally unique across every loaded tool-provider —
        /// the registry's conflict policy (sidecar `pool_priority`
        /// ascending, first-wins) decides which plugin owns the
        /// name when two declare it. Examples: `"math-eval"`,
        /// `"datetime-arith"`, `"unit-convert"`.
        name: string,

        /// Human-readable description shown to the LLM as the
        /// tool's `description` field. Should explain when to use
        /// the tool, NOT how — argument shape is the json-schema's
        /// job.
        description: string,

        /// JSON Schema for the `args-json` payload. The host
        /// passes this verbatim to the LLM. Keep it minimal —
        /// `{"type":"object","properties":{"expr":{"type":"string"}},
        /// "required":["expr"]}` is plenty for a math-eval tool.
        json-schema: string,
    }

    /// Successful tool invocation result. `json-output` is the
    /// canonical-JSON the LLM consumes verbatim through the
    /// `tool-result` content-block in `wild:ai/chat`.
    record tool-result {
        /// JSON the LLM sees. Plain strings are valid JSON; numeric
        /// outputs are JSON numbers. Whatever shape, the consumer
        /// (LLM tool-loop) decides how to render it.
        json-output: string,

        /// Optional cost-units the host can sum across a session
        /// for budget tracking. Tool authors emit `Some(n)` when a
        /// tool has a meaningful cost (web-search API call, S3
        /// GET) and `None` for free pure-eval tools.
        cost-units: option<u64>,
    }

    /// The failure CLASS of a `fault` (ADR-0116 D1) — closed and
    /// enumerable so the host waist can route on it instead of
    /// parsing prose. A tool that cannot tell its class keeps
    /// emitting `execution-failed` (the host maps it to
    /// `unknown`); teaching a tool its class is an on-touch
    /// migration, never a requirement.
    ///
    /// - `auth`         — the remote rejected the credential
    ///                    (missing / wrong / expired / revoked;
    ///                    HTTP 401/403). Operator-actionable.
    /// - `config`       — the component's own configuration is
    ///                    wrong (bad endpoint, missing mapping).
    /// - `unreachable`  — the remote can't be reached or answered
    ///                    with a non-auth failure (DNS, connect,
    ///                    5xx).
    /// - `rate-limited` — throttled (HTTP 429); a backoff
    ///                    policy's lane.
    /// - `transient`    — expected to pass on its own; retry lane.
    /// - `user-error`   — the CALLER supplied bad input; stays
    ///                    with the caller, never routed out.
    /// - `unknown`      — the component can't classify it.
    enum fault-class {
        auth,
        config,
        unreachable,
        rate-limited,
        transient,
        user-error,
        unknown,
    }

    /// One classified fault — the typed alternative to prose in
    /// `execution-failed`. `message` stays the human detail; the
    /// class is what routes (ADR-0116 D4/D5).
    record fault-detail {
        class: fault-class,
        message: string,
    }

    /// Typed failure modes. The host's tool-loop decides how to
    /// surface each one to the LLM:
    ///
    /// - `unknown-tool`     — registry routing bug; never reaches
    ///                        the plugin in practice (registry has
    ///                        already resolved the name). Defensive.
    /// - `invalid-args`     — args-json doesn't match the schema.
    ///                        Surfaced to the LLM as a tool-result
    ///                        with `is_error: true` so it can retry
    ///                        with corrected args.
    /// - `execution-failed` — the tool ran but its work failed
    ///                        (network down, division-by-zero in a
    ///                        math eval, missing secret) and the
    ///                        tool can't say which class — prose
    ///                        only. Same as invalid-args from the
    ///                        LLM's POV.
    /// - `timeout`          — host-enforced timeout on the WIT call.
    ///                        Surfaced as `is_error: true`.
    /// - `fault`            — the tool ran, failed, AND knows the
    ///                        failure class (ADR-0116 D1). Same as
    ///                        execution-failed from the LLM's POV;
    ///                        the class exists for the host-side
    ///                        fault net, not the model.
    variant tool-error {
        unknown-tool(string),
        invalid-args(string),
        execution-failed(string),
        timeout,
        fault(fault-detail),
    }

    /// One Skill MD bundled with the plugin. The host registers
    /// these into the per-tribe `skills` table at component-load
    /// time so the rest of the runtime can resolve them through
    /// the same Skill registry it uses for prompts/skills, MCP
    /// adapters and forge output (see `docs/skill-vs-tool.md`).
    ///
    /// One MD per Tool the plugin exports is the canonical shape:
    /// it lets a Tool's worked examples + role hints + version
    /// live structurally next to source instead of being smuggled
    /// through `tool-spec.description`. A plugin that ships no
    /// MDs at all is fine — the registry treats `list-skill-mds`
    /// as a hint, never a requirement.
    record skill-md {
        /// Stable Skill slug. Lower-kebab-case, globally unique
        /// across the registry. By convention identical to the
        /// Tool name when the Skill wraps a single Tool — but
        /// this is not enforced (composite Skills wrap several
        /// Tools or none). The host registers
        /// `<slug>` into the `skills` table verbatim; collisions
        /// resolve through the registry's per-source policy.
        slug: string,

        /// Markdown body, including YAML frontmatter. The host
        /// parses the frontmatter (`SkillSpec` shape — slug,
        /// version, source, roles, examples) and stores the
        /// body verbatim. Plugins should `include_str!` this
        /// from a sibling `.md` file so `cargo component build`
        /// bundles it into the Wasm component.
        body: string,
    }

    /// Return the catalog this plugin exposes. Called once at host
    /// boot per pooled instance to populate the registry. The
    /// returned list MUST be deterministic across calls within one
    /// plugin lifecycle — the host caches the boot-time catalog
    /// and uses it for routing dispatch.
    list-tools: func() -> list<tool-spec>;

    /// Return Skill MDs bundled with the plugin. Called once at
    /// component-load time alongside `list-tools()`; the host
    /// registers each MD into the per-tribe Skill registry.
    /// Plugins MUST return an empty list if they ship no MDs —
    /// callers do not synthesise Skills from Tool specs alone.
    /// Like `list-tools()`, the result MUST be deterministic
    /// across calls within one plugin lifecycle.
    list-skill-mds: func() -> list<skill-md>;

    /// Dispatch a tool call. `name` is one of the names returned
    /// by `list-tools`; `args-json` is the LLM-emitted argument
    /// bundle. The plugin parses `args-json` against its own
    /// schema for `name`, runs the tool, and returns either a
    /// `tool-result` or a typed `tool-error`.
    ///
    /// `async func` (ADR-0100): the host drives it under `run_concurrent`,
    /// so a tool may `.await` a component-model-async import — draining a
    /// `wild:host-source/reader` / `wild:files/reader` `stream<u8>` to decode
    /// a large source in place instead of buying the whole `list<u8>` by
    /// value. A tool that awaits nothing (the common case) is unaffected —
    /// the async binding just resolves immediately.
    invoke: async func(name: string, args-json: string) -> result<tool-result, tool-error>;
}

// ADR-0100 D4 — native streaming decode. Where a format adapter's
// `invoke("<x>-decode", {range:{offset,limit}})` returns ONE row-range as JSON
// and the intake runner poll-loops it (re-parsing the whole source on every
// chunk — O(files x rows^2)), `decode-stream` returns ALL rows as a native
// `stream<row>` the consumer drains once: no poll loop, no re-parse.
//
// `async func`: the host drives it under `run_concurrent`; the guest produces
// via `wit_stream::new()` + `spawn_local(write_all)` (needs wit-bindgen
// `async-spawn`). ADDITIVE on the unpublished `@0.3.0` (no bump — like
// `wild:host-source/reader` at `@0.1.0`); a format adapter opts in by exporting
// `decoder` in its world. Only reachable through the host relay
// (`wild:tools/invoke-stream`), never the LLM tool catalog.
interface decoder {
    /// One decoded row — canonical-string cells, the same shape as the poll
    /// path's `rows[]` entries. A named `record` so the stream element is typed.
    record row {
        cells: list<string>,
    }

    /// Stream every decoded row for tool `name` with `args-json` (the same
    /// decode args as `invoke`, minus `range` — the stream replaces the cursor).
    /// On the error path the string carries the underlying tool-error detail.
    decode-stream: async func(name: string, args-json: string) -> result<stream<row>, string>;
}

// World template (documented, not parsed):
//
// Tool-pack authors define their own world based on what the pack
// needs to import. The package intentionally exposes only the
// `tools` interface so adding a new pack with different imports
// (web-search needing wasi:http, s3 needing wild:blobs +
// wild:secrets) doesn't require a WIT bump.
//
// Pure-eval pack (math-tools, the W2 reference):
//
//   world math-tools {
//       include wild:plugin-meta/plugin-base@0.3.0;
//       export wild:tool-provider/tools@0.4.0;
//   }
//
// HTTP-binding pack (web-search-tools, future):
//
//   world web-search-tools {
//       include wild:plugin-meta/plugin-base@0.3.0;
//       import wasi:http/outgoing-handler@0.2.0;
//       import wild:secrets/store@0.1.0;
//       export wild:tool-provider/tools@0.4.0;
//   }
//
// Each import the world declares lands in the plugin manifest's
// `requires: []` list and is checked by the D3 Stage-2 trust gate
// at load time before the plugin is admitted.

// The host side of the contract — the deps-free worlds the linker
// `bindgen!`s (`crates/runtime/wild-host/src/shims/tool_provider*`).
// A tool-provider guest declares its OWN world (`include plugin-base;
// export tools;`); these host worlds carry no `plugin-base`/`secrets`/
// `http` imports, so they resolve inside the host WIT tree without the
// foreign packages. The host copy under
// `crates/runtime/wild-host/wit/wild/` is GENERATED from this file
// (`xtask wit-host-write`): guest worlds are dropped, these host worlds
// are kept.
world tool-provider-host {
    export tools;
}
world tool-provider-decoder-host {
    export decoder;
}
